I'm a long time D&D player/DM, and I have a slightly different take on
alignments, specifically CE, perhaps with a bit of documentation,
even. I'll type in the relevant sections from the D&D Player's
Handbook (3.5, FWIW) to try and add more clarity. I know NHIND&D, but
this conversation appears to have moved a bit away from that anyway.

"'Evil' implies hurting, oppressing, and killing others. Some evil
creatures simply have no compassion for other and kill without qualms
if doing so is convenient. Others actively pursue evil, killing for
sport or out of duty to some evil deity or master."

"'Chaos' implies freedom, adaptability, and flexibility. On the
downside, chaos can include recklessness, resentment towards
legitimate authority, arbitrary actions, and irresponsibility. Those
who promote chaotic behavior say that only unfettered personal freedom
allows people to express themselves fully and lets society benefit
from the potential that its individuals have within them"

"Chaotic Evil, 'Destroyer': A chaotic evil character does whatever
greed, hatred, and lust for destruction drive him to do. He is hot
tempered, vicious, arbitrarily violent, and unpredictable. If he is
simply out for whatever he can get, he is ruthless and brutal. If he
is committed to the spread of evil and chaos, he is even worse.
Thankfully, his plans are haphazard, and any groups he joins or forms
are poorly organized. Typically, chaotic evil people can be made to
work only by force, and their leader lasts only as long as he can
thwart attempts to topple and assassinate him."

And what does this tell us (other than the fact that I have too much
free time and, now, rather cramped hands from leaning down and typing
simultaneously)? Any ethical alignment (Lawful - Neutral - Chaotic)
can be Hellbent on turning the world into an evil place, and chaotics
may wish for it to be a chaotic one. Almost none of them are just
doing evil for evil's sake, though, and even the ones that are are
doing it for some form of personal gain. (Demon Prince: I'll give you
the world if you send it to Hell in a Handbasket; CE Orc: Okay . . .)
Lawful characters are unlikely to want the world to be chaotic, as
they will then be unable to control it (the Mafia would be Lawful
Evil) and Chaotics are incapable of the order necessary to accomplish
it, even if they want it (and not all do . . . um, think of . . .
Anarchist gangs?).

So where is the hope for a world wracked with chaos, you may ask? The
lowly Neutral Evil, so often ignored, is, in fact, that alignment most
likely to accomplish this task. They possess the ability to work with
others, and, more importantly, under others, when necessary, but are
not bound by laws and have no reason to wish to maintain them.

So, um, what was I talking about? Ah, yes, in closing, Evil is,
inherently, out for itself,  Lawful/Chaotic aside. A disregard for
personal safety has more to do with intelligence (or lack thereof)
instead of alignment, so the orcs in your example are just stupid, my
most esteemed Artemis.

